fof_version: '1.0'
type: enemy
id: latchling
name: Latchling
ruleset:
  modules:
  - id: base
    version: 1.0.0
enemy:
  # v0.3: Mook, attack +1, heavy armor, rating 5 (the armored Mook that needed a 10+).
  level: 2
  role: mook
  armor: 2
  morale: 12
  weapon: One bolt-arm; the other arm is a hand
  wants: Nobody through its doorway without the right token.
  special: BRASS — a latchling drops only to a hit of 10+. On a 7–9 the blow rings off it
    and it is still in the doorway.
  when_bloodied: null
  tells: Something small and brass sits in the exact middle of a doorway, and the dust around
    it has not been disturbed in years.
  breaks: Cannot be frightened. It steps aside, without a roll and without resentment, for
    correct authority (a seal, a phrase, a livery), and it stops for good when the door is
    no longer a door.
  twists:
  - The token it wants is hanging on a hook in plain sight, on the wrong side of the door.
  - Three latchlings keep three doors, and each accepts a different livery.
  - The door it keeps was bricked up long ago. It keeps the bricks.
  - It is keeping the cupboard the party's confiscated gear was locked in.
  - 'It is jammed half-open: each exchange it can bar the way or hit, not both.'
  - Someone in the building has been handing it buttons, and it now accepts buttons as authority.
  nastier: Its doorway leads to a latchman's door, and the latchman hears the brass ring.
  description: >-
    Knee-high, brass-jointed, and built to sit in a doorway and be a nuisance to anyone without
    the right token. A latchling has one arm that is a bolt and one that is a hand, and it
    is very clear about which is for whom.
  notes: >-
    Level 2 Mook, armor 2. The v0.3 armored-Mook rule survives as its special, because under
    the lean rules a Mook drops to any hit and armor alone would not make a latchling any
    different from a chicken.


    It never leaves its doorway: not to chase, not to help another latchling, not for anything.
    Its first target is whoever is nearest the door it keeps. It would much rather block than
    hit.


    Appears: one per doorway, in buildings that once had rules.
  reskin_of: null
